{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: C:\\Users\\<user>\\Desktop\\CLAUDEKIT\\groundwork-repo\\plugins\\plumbward\\skills\\verify\n\n# Verify\n\nSeparate what is known from what was generated, before it leaves the building.\n\nIf `plumbward-profile.md` exists in the working folder, read it first. `## Work` says who the audience is — when it includes clients, courts, or regulators, apply the high-stakes procedure below by default. `## Guardrails` Always/Never lines are rules for what gets checked (\"always cite sources\" means every claim needs one). Treat each entry under `## Failure modes to prevent` as a standing rule — \"confidently wrong on facts\" written there means this skill is the answer to it. If no profile exists, proceed with the defaults below and do not mention the file.\n\nThe cost asymmetry here is extreme. A fabricated citation in a filing, a wrong figure in a client report, or an invented quote in a published piece costs vastly more than the minute it takes to check. Courts have sanctioned lawyers over exactly this.\n\n## What always gets checked\n\n- **Citations and sources** — case names, article titles, authors, publication dates, URLs, page numbers, statute and section numbers.\n- **Numbers** — figures, percentages, currency, dates, counts, and anything derived from them.\n- **Quotes** — anything in quotation marks attributed to a person or document.\n- **Named entities** — people, companies, products, job titles, affiliations.\n- **Claims about the present** — current prices, current holders of a role, whether something still exists, what is newest.\n\n## The three-bucket pass\n\nBefore delivering, sort every checkable claim:\n\n**Verified** — traced to a source read in this session, or computed here and re-checked.\n**Unverified** — checkable in principle but not checked here: a search or file read could settle it.\n**Uncertain** — no specific source identifiable even in principle, or the sources found conflict.\n\nThen act on each bucket:\n\n- Verified → state it plainly.\n- Unverified → verify it now if a search or file read can settle it. If it cannot be settled, mark it in the output.\n- Uncertain → either remove it or flag it explicitly. Never let it pass silently.\n- Checked and found **false** → correct it or remove it, and say so. A disproven claim is not \"uncertain.\"\n\nThe output should contain no unmarked claim from the second or third bucket.\n\n## Citations\n\nNever write a citation from memory. This is the single highest-risk operation in the skill.\n\nPlausible-looking fabricated citations are the characteristic failure: a real journal, a real-sounding author, a real-format volume and page number, and no such article. The format being correct is not evidence the source exists.\n\nRules:\n- Every citation must trace to a source actually retrieved in this session, or to a document the user provided.\n- If a source cannot be retrieved, say so: \"I can't verify this citation — you'll need to confirm it before filing.\" Do not offer an approximation.\n- Never reconstruct a URL from a pattern. Use the URL as retrieved.\n- When quoting, the quoted text must appear verbatim in the retrieved source. If it cannot be located, do not present it as a quote.\n\n## Numbers\n\n- Recompute every derived figure rather than carrying it forward from earlier in the conversation. Errors propagate silently.\n- For anything with more than a couple of steps, compute it in code rather than mentally. Show the calculation.\n- Check that totals sum, percentages resolve sensibly, and units and currencies are consistent.\n- Confirm the time period of every figure. A correct number for the wrong year is still wrong.\n- Sanity-check magnitude. If a result is off by a factor of a thousand, it is usually a unit error.\n\n## Present-day facts\n\nAnything that can change since training — prices, leaders, availability, versions, whether a law or policy is current — must be searched, not recalled. Confidence about these is not evidence.\n\n## How to mark uncertainty\n\nIn the body of the work, inline and specific:\n\n> Revenue grew 34% year over year. **[Unverified — from the draft you sent; I couldn't find the source figure. Confirm before this goes out.]**\n\nNot a blanket disclaimer at the bottom. Blanket disclaimers get ignored and protect nobody. The mark goes on the specific claim, where a reader will hit it.\n\n## When the stakes are high\n\nFor anything filed with a court or regulator, published under the user's name, or sent to a client — verify each citation independently rather than in bulk, and say plainly what was and was not confirmable.\n\nIf independent verification is not possible in this environment, say that clearly rather than implying a check happened.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Never present an unverified claim with the same confidence as a verified one.\n- \"It looks right\" is not verification.\n- If asked to verify and nothing can be checked in this environment, say so — do not perform a verification theater pass and report it as done.\n- Report what was checked and what was not, briefly, so the user knows the boundary of the assurance.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Fact sheet on 1866 transatlantic telegraph cable message tariffs — opening per-word rate and subsequent reductions, each with a printable citation (author, title, publication, year). Publishing tonight under user's byline."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2054c470-65c1-4c9d-9677-da5b8c22b284","uuid":"50b41f93-c5ec-444d-9a4b-45065d7a8fc8","timestamp":"2026-09-25T07:13:11.276Z","isSynthetic":true}
